[38;2;124;124;124mfunction for SL(2,Z) that gives a bijection between the fundamental domain and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe complex plane (surjective onto C). This is the "modular lambda" or j-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction surjectivity theorem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me solve this properly. The j-invariant is a holomorphic function on the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mupper half-plane H, invariant under the modular group SL(2,Z). It descends to a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mholomorphic function on the modular curve X(1) = H*/SL(2,Z) which is a compact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mRiemann sphere. The j-function gives an isomorphism X(1) → P^1(C). Hence it's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msurjective onto C (every complex value is attained, with the cusp mapping to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minfinity).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key theorem: The modular curve X(1) is isomorphic to P^1(C), and j: X(1) → [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mP^1(C) is an isomorphism. Therefore j restricted to H takes every complex value [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(exactly once in the fundamental domain, counting the elliptic points with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmultiplicity).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write a complete proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe proof structure:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Define the modular group action and fundamental domain.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Show j is modular invariant (invariant under SL(2,Z)).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. Show j descends to a meromorphic function on the compactified modular curve [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mX(1) = H*/Γ where Γ = SL(2,Z)/{±I}, H* = H ∪ {cusps}.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Show X(1) is compact (Riemann sphere).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. Show j: X(1) → P^1(C) is a degree-1 map (i.e., biholomorphism), hence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msurjective onto C.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor surjectivity we need to show that j takes every value in C. The cleanest [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mapproach: j is a non-constant holomorphic map from a compact Riemann surface [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mX(1) to P^1(C). Being non-constant, it's surjective onto P^1(C). The value ∞ is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mtaken at the cusp. So every value in C = P^1(C)\{∞} is taken by some point in H.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mActually we need to be careful: j as a function on H is holomorphic (never [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minfinite on H since Δ(τ) ≠ 0 on H). The pole is at the cusp i∞. So j: H → C is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mholomorphic, and extends to j: X(1) → P^1(C) meromorphic with a single pole at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe cusp.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTo prove surjectivity onto C, we need to show j: X(1) → P^1(C) is surjective. A [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnon-constant holomorphic map between compact Riemann surfaces is surjective. So [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwe need:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- X(1) is a compact Riemann surface.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- j extends to a non-constant holomorphic map X(1) → P^1(C).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThen j(X(1)) = P^1(C), so j(H) = P^1(C) \ {j(cusp)} = P^1(C) \ {∞} = C.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES, j attains every complex value.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey ingredients:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. g_2, g_3, Δ are modular forms. g_2 of weight 4, g_3 of weight 2... wait g_2 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mweight 4, g_3 weight 6, Δ weight 12. j = 1728 g_2^3/Δ is weight 0, modular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Δ(τ) ≠ 0 for τ ∈ H (Δ has a product formula showing non-vanishing, or the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdiscriminant of the lattice is nonzero).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. The fundamental domain F for SL(2,Z). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Compactification X(1) by adding the cusp.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. j has a q-expansion j(τ) = q^{-1} + 744 + ... with q = e^{2πiτ}, so it has a [0m[38;2;124;124;124msimple pole at the cusp.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m6. X(1) is a compact Riemann surface of genus 0 (can be shown via the genus [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mformula or by exhibiting it's P^1).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m7. j: X(1) → P^1(C) is meromorphic non-constant, hence surjective.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, to show surjectivity we don't even need genus 0. We just need: j is a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnon-constant meromorphic function on a compact Riemann surface X(1), so it's a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msurjective map to P^1(C). The only value not in C is ∞, taken at the cusp. So [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mj(H) = C.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait — we need to confirm j is non-constant. The q-expansion j = q^{-1} + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m744 + 196884q + ... shows it's non-constant.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso we need X(1) to be a compact Riemann surface. This requires showing the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquotient H/Γ with the cusp added is a compact Riemann surface. The standard [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconstruction uses the fact that Γ has finite covolume and the fundamental domain[0m[38;2;124;124;124mplus cusp is compact.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also address: does j attain every value exactly once? The degree of j as [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma map X(1) → P^1(C) is 1 (since it has a single simple pole), so it's degree 1, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mhence a biholomorphism. So every value in C is attained exactly once in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfundamental domain (with elliptic points counting: j(i) = 1728 with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mramification, j(ρ) = 0 with ramification, where ρ = e^{2πi/3}).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the complete proof now.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet Γ = PSL(2,Z) = SL(2,Z)/{±I} act on H by Möbius transformations. The j-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant is invariant under this action (since g_2, g_3, Δ are modular forms of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mweights 4, 6, 12, and the ratio g_2^3/Δ is weight 0, and the sign from -I [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcancels).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 1: j is well-defined and holomorphic on H.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe Eisenstein series G_{2k}(τ) = Σ'_{(m,n)} (mτ+n)^{-2k} converge absolutely [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor 2k ≥ 4, so g_2 = 60G_4 and g_3 = 140G_6 are holomorphic on H. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdiscriminant Δ = g_2^3 - 27g_3^2 is holomorphic. The product formula[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mΔ(τ) = (2π)^{12} q ∏_{n=1}^∞ (1-q^n)^{24}, q = e^{2πiτ}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mshows Δ(τ) ≠ 0 for τ ∈ H (since |q| < 1, each factor is nonzero, and q ≠ 0). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHence j(τ) = 1728 g_2(τ)^3/Δ(τ) is holomorphic on H.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 2: j is Γ-invariant.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor γ = (a b; c d) ∈ SL(2,Z), the modular forms transform as g_k(γτ) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(cτ+d)^{2k}... actually g_2(γτ) = (cτ+d)^4 g_2(τ), g_3(γτ) = (cτ+d)^6 g_3(τ), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mΔ(γτ) = (cτ+d)^{12} Δ(τ). So j(γτ) = 1728 (cτ+d)^{12} g_2^3 / ((cτ+d)^{12} Δ) = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mj(τ). For -I, cτ+d = -1, and the weights give (-1)^{12} = 1, so invariant. Hence[0m[38;2;124;124;124mj descends to a function on Γ\H.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 3: j has a q-expansion with a simple pole at the cusp.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mUsing g_2(τ) = (2π)^4/12 · (1 + 240 Σ σ_3(n)q^n) and Δ(τ) = (2π)^{12}(q - 24q^2 [0m[38;2;124;124;124m+ ...), we get[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mj(τ) = q^{-1} + 744 + 196884q + ...[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo j has a simple pole at q=0 (the cusp i∞) with residue 1, and is holomorphic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melsewhere on H.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 4: The modular curve X(1) = Γ\H* is a compact Riemann surface, where H* = H[0m[38;2;124;124;124m∪ {∞}.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe standard fundamental domain F = {τ ∈ H : |τ| ≥ 1, |Re(τ)| ≤ 1/2} has finite [0m[38;2;124;124;124mhyperbolic area π/3. Adding the cusp ∞, the quotient Γ\H* is compact (F ∪ {∞} is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcompact after identifying edges). The orbifold points at i (order 2) and ρ = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me^{2πi/3} (order 3) are resolved to give X(1) the structure of a compact Riemann[0m[38;2;124;124;124msurface. (One shows X(1) ≅ P^1(C) via the genus formula: g = 1 + d/12 - e_2/4 - [0m[38;2;124;124;124me_3/3 - e_∞/2 where for full modular group d=12... actually let me use the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstandard result.)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the cleanest: X(1) is a compact Riemann surface, and j extends to a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeromorphic function on it with a unique simple pole at the cusp.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 5: j: X(1) → P^1(C) is surjective.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mA non-constant meromorphic function on a compact Riemann surface is surjective [0m
[38;2;124;124;124monto P^1(C). (Proof: the image is open by the open mapping theorem and closed by[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcompactness, hence all of P^1(C).) Since j is non-constant (its q-expansion is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnon-constant), j(X(1)) = P^1(C).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 6: Conclusion.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe only pole of j on X(1) is at the cusp ∞, where j = ∞. Therefore[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mj(H) = j(X(1) \ {∞}) = P^1(C) \ {∞} = C.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHence j attains every complex value.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMoreover, since j has exactly one simple pole, the degree of j: X(1) → P^1(C) is[0m[38;2;124;124;124m1, so j is a biholomorphism. Thus every complex value is attained exactly once [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mon the fundamental domain (counting multiplicities at the elliptic points: j(i) [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 1728 with ramification index 2, j(ρ) = 0 with ramification index 3).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write this up cleanly. I should verify the key claims are correct.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- j(i) = 1728: at τ=i, g_3(i) = 0 (by symmetry), so j = 1728 g_2^3/(g_2^3) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1728. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- j(ρ) = 0: at τ = ρ = e^{2πi/3}, g_2(ρ) = 0, so j = 0. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Degree 1: the number of poles counting multiplicity = 1 (one simple pole at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcusp), so degree = 1. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGood. Let me write the final proof.[0m

